# tests/logos/unregistered.ledger — the corpus files that are in NO suite.
#
# WHAT REGISTERS A TEST. `tests/logos/CMakeLists.txt` globs `*.expected` and
# derives the `.logos` from it. So a `.logos` with no sibling `.expected` is
# registered nowhere — and unlike the reverse case (an `.expected` with no
# `.logos`, which cmake reports with `message(WARNING …)` and skips) NOTHING
# says so. The file sits in the corpus directory, `test-levels.sh` selects its
# name, ctest does not know it, and every level reports a pass count that is a
# true statement about a smaller suite.
#
# THE CHECK RUNS BOTH WAYS, like `tests/exhaustive/refusals.ledger`. An
# unlisted orphan fails the gate — it is a new one. A listed path that now HAS
# its `.expected`, or that no longer exists, ALSO fails — the arc landed and the
# ledger has stopped describing the corpus. There is no way to make the gate
# green by adding a line and walking away.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE IS A RECORD OF WORK NOT DONE, NOT A PERMISSION. Every line below
# is a test somebody wrote and nobody runs. Two of them additionally record a
# compiler answer that is not the one the file was written to assert, and those
# are the reason this is a ledger rather than fourteen new `.expected` files
# written from what the compiler prints today — a `.expected` derived from the
# implementation is the implementation checking itself.
#
# ── tests/logos/fail — the compiler REFUSES these, so an `.expected` naming the
#    diagnostic would register them as-is. Measured 2026-07-31: each exits
#    non-zero on `logosc <f> -o /dev/null`.
tests/logos/fail/naming_collision.logos
tests/logos/fail/trait_unsafe_mismatch.logos
tests/logos/fail/unchecked_struct_bounds.logos
tests/logos/fail/variadic_bounds_repro.logos
tests/logos/fail/variadic_collision.logos
#
# ── tests/logos/fail — THE COMPILER DOES NOT REFUSE THESE. Measured
#    2026-07-31: `logosc <f> -o /dev/null` exits 0 on both, so registering them
#    as fail tests would be registering a red gate.
#    * assoc_primitive — `fn check_i32(val: i32::Item) -> bool` compiles. An
#      associated-type projection through a PRIMITIVE self type is accepted.
#    * simple — `package test; struct S { x: i32 } fn main() {}` compiles, and
#      should: it is a pass-shaped file sitting in fail/.
tests/logos/fail/assoc_primitive.logos
tests/logos/fail/simple.logos
#
# ── tests/logos/pass — these compile and run; they need `exit:`/`stdout:`
#    expectations that state what each one is FOR, not what it happens to print.
tests/logos/pass/assoc_proj_in_default_method.logos
tests/logos/pass/default_type_param_fn.logos
tests/logos/pass/lforge_manifest.logos
tests/logos/pass/struct_lifetime_generic.logos
tests/logos/pass/struct_lifetime_param.logos
#
# ── tests/imported — Rust-derived conformance ports that arrived without an
#    expectation.
tests/imported/pass/closures/hrtb-deep-bound-chain.logos
tests/imported/pass/late-bound-lifetimes/issue-36381.logos
